<ResourceDictionary xmlns:x="http://adamantium/ui/xaml/extensions"
        xmlns="http://adamantium/ui">

    <!-- The macOS theme's transient surfaces, and they are LIQUID GLASS rather than acrylic.

         THE KEYS ARE THE SHARED ONES. FlyoutSurfaceFill names a ROLE - "the surface a menu, a drop-down, a ribbon
         flyout or a slide panel is made of" - and three of those sets are still Fluent's own. Answering the key here is
         what lets the whole theme change material without touching a line of them. (They were called AcrylicFlyoutFill
         and AcrylicTooltipFill until this went in, which put the MATERIAL in the contract and left this file either
         lying in the name or re-templating four borrowed controls.)

         WHAT ACTUALLY CHANGES. Acrylic is a BLUR plus a tint - it hides what is behind it and reads as frosted plastic.
         Liquid glass BENDS what is behind it instead: the capture is sampled with an offset that grows towards the
         edge, so the shape reads as a thick drop with a lens at its rim, with a faint colour fringe where the bending
         is strongest and a bright line along the turn. In the middle it is nearly clear.

         TWO CONSEQUENCES WORTH KNOWING BEFORE JUDGING IT:

         THE NUMBERS MOVED WITH THE FILTER. They were 3 and 2 against the old kernel, where the value was the outermost
         tap's radius and the kernel fell apart past about three. It is now a radius in LOGICAL pixels, realised through
         the capture's pyramid, and honest up to sixty-four device pixels - so the same look needs a far larger number.

         BlurAmount now DOES something: above zero the pass blurs what the flat middle transmits - through the same
         Gaussian the frosted pass uses - and leaves the rim sharp. Zero skips it entirely. The Brushes stand carries
         the slider; these numbers are where the line was drawn between legible and still-glass.

         And the tint is CAPPED AT HALF by the material itself (the pass multiplies the alpha by 0.5, because glass that
         takes a heavy tint is a panel again). So 1.0 here is not "opaque", it is the most tint this material admits -
         which is still a good deal clearer than the acrylic it replaces. If a menu over a busy white document turns out
         to be hard to read, that is this trade showing, and going back is one attribute. -->

    <!-- The flyout surface: menus, submenus, drop-down lists, the ribbon's flyout, a slide panel.

         THE TINT IS TAKEN LIVE, with {ObservableResource} and not {ResourceReference}: a static reference resolves once,
         and a brush does not live in the visual tree, so these surfaces kept the colour of the variant they were built
         under and stayed dark through every switch to light while the solid fills around them followed. Gradient stops
         fell into the same trap and this is the same cure.

         Refraction is the material's OWN default, and left there deliberately after a spell at 8: the strength is the
         displacement at the very edge in device pixels, and the lens band it drives reaches twice that inwards. At 8
         the band is 16px, which on a menu is a hairline nobody reads as glass; at 12 it is 24 and the bend is a thing
         you can see. It only shows where there is something BEHIND to bend, which is the honest limit of this material
         and not a number to chase: over a flat panel a lens and a blur both leave a flat panel. -->
    <MaterialBrush x:Key="FlyoutSurfaceFill"
                   Material="LiquidGlass"
                   TintColor="{ObservableResource AcrylicFillColorDefault}"
                   TintOpacity="1.0"
                   BlurAmount="5"
                   Refraction="12"
                   NoiseAmount="0.02"/>

    <!-- A tooltip is small, and the lens is measured in device pixels from the edge - at the flyout's strength the
         bending would reach the middle of it and there would be no flat part left to read text on. -->
    <MaterialBrush x:Key="TooltipSurfaceFill"
                   Material="LiquidGlass"
                   TintColor="{ObservableResource AcrylicFillColorDefault}"
                   TintOpacity="1.0"
                   BlurAmount="4"
                   Refraction="7"
                   NoiseAmount="0.02"/>

</ResourceDictionary>
